    "breadcrumb": "Commodity Recognition Book",
    "content": "The appearance of cocoa in satellite imagery is as diverse as the farmers who produce it: cocoa is grown in over sixty countries across the tropics by an estimated 5–6 million farmers, the majority of whom are smallholders. This diversity, the small plot typical plot size, and the fact that cocoa is often planted irregularly under shade trees can make it difficult to identify in remote sensing data. This section will explore the production system characteristics that impact the appearance of cocoa in satellite imagery, and provide guidelines and examples to help differentiate cocoa from other crops and from native forest.\nCocoa GalleryLearn to recognize cocoa in satellite imagery.\nUnderstanding Cocoa CultivationLearn how cocoa is grown and why it looks the way it does from above.",
