    "breadcrumb": "Commodity Recognition Book \u003e Cocoa Recognition \u003e Cocoa Gallery \u003e Cocoa “Look-Alike” Crops",
    "content": "This page is in flux; do not copy edit yet. The content is copied from the corresponding coffee page. Some crops are grown using vertical support structures—such as poles, wires, or trellises—which create regular and distinctive patterns in high-resolution satellite imagery. These crops can easily be confused with coffee when viewed from above, especially when intercropped or when their planting density is high. Recognizing the linear or grid-like arrangements, along with shadow patterns, is key to distinguishing them.\nBlack pepper Black pepper cultivation is characterized by several distinct features. The vine is typically grown on poles or trees, resulting in a recognizable pattern of vertical structures. These poles or trees, often part of agroforestry systems, are interspersed with shade trees, creating a heterogeneous canopy cover. The structured arrangement of the poles or trees often forms clear and repeating planting patterns visible in imagery. Shadows cast by the supporting structures further enhance the visibility of these patterns, particularly in high-resolution imagery. Additionally, black pepper is frequently intercropped with coffee, this can be identified as the shorter and denser coffee plants contrast with the vertical growth of pepper vines.\nDragon fruit Dragon fruit, also known as Pitaya, is a perennial, epiphytic, vine-like cactus fruit tree that exhibits distinct features observable in remote sensing. Like black pepper, dragon fruit is typically grown on poles, resulting in a very clear and organized planting pattern. The vertical structure of the poles, along with the shadows they cast, serves as a key indicator that distinguishes dragon fruit plantations from coffee plantations in high-resolution imagery.\nPassion Fruit Passion fruit is commonly grown on trellises or wire supports stretched between wooden or metal posts. These installations form regular, linear patterns that are easily spotted in satellite imagery, especially when rows are aligned and evenly spaced. The canopy itself may appear relatively uniform and medium in height, sometimes resembling young coffee in compact plots. However, the visible support structures and row spacing can help distinguish passion fruit plantations from coffee systems.\nTable. Trellised Crops idlatlondescriptionimagery dateslink imagestreetview linkgoogle earth linkgoogle maps link Passion fruit with a typical grid structure. Historical imagery from 2021 shows that black pepper was previously growing on this plot. 1 12.22063 107.514705 Passion fruit 14/01/2021 View in Google Earth View in Google Maps Black pepper, likely growing on living trees. Black pepper seems to be intercropped with coffee in the plots around the one at the center of the image. 2 11.61447765 108.1603979 Black pepper 27/12/2019 View in Google Earth View in Google Maps Dragon fruits grow on artificial poles that are further apart than black pepper or coffee trees. These poles are often organized in a very regular grid. 22 11.11217502 108.1641781 Dragon fruit 25/08/2024 View in Street View View in Google Earth View in Google Maps Dragon fruits grow on artificial poles that are further apart than black pepper or coffee trees. These poles are often organized in a very regular grid. 23 10.99078127 108.1034389 Dragon fruit 4/3/24 View in Street View View in Google Earth View in Google Maps Dragon fruits grow on artificial poles that are further apart than black pepper or coffee trees. These poles are often organized in a very regular grid. 88 -4.3775041 120.0884512 Dragon fruit tree 22/02/2025 View in Street View View in Google Earth View in Google Maps Dragon fruits grow on artificial poles that are further apart than black pepper or coffee trees. These poles are often organized in a very regular grid. 89 -4.3653509 120.0700276 Dragon fruit tree 22/02/2025 View in Street View View in Google Earth View in Google Maps",
