    "breadcrumb": "Commodity Recognition Book \u003e Coffee Recognition \u003e Understanding Coffee Cultivation",
    "content": "Overview The life cycle of a coffee tree begins in a nursery before being transplanted into the field. Once planted, it typically takes 3 to 4 years for the young coffee trees to mature and start bearing fruit. During this maturation period, coffee is often intercropped with other commercially valuable crops such as banana, plantain, or passion fruit vines. These companion crops provide shade and generate income for farmers while the coffee trees develop.\nUpon reaching maturity, coffee trees are usually pruned annually after the harvest to maintain their structure and productivity, although the frequency of this practice is not universal.\nAfter nine to ten years or as early as five to six years in Colombia coffee trees are commonly stumped, cutting back the trunk to its base to encourage regrowth and to sustain high yields. This rejuvenation process takes about a year. After approximately twenty years, the productivity of coffee trees declines significantly, prompting their removal and replacement with new seedlings.\nExamples idlatlontopiccoffee planting patternshade planting patternshade levelagepruningvarietyintercropirrigationspecial patternslope artifactimagery datestreetview linkgoogle earth linkgoogle maps linkcountry Coffee with medium shade level. Historical images on Google Earth show that the shade trees were planted at the same time as the coffee. A plot with denser shade is visible about 50m north of the marker, where coffee is still visible between the shade trees, although the canopy is nearly closed. 3 14.50101516 -89.16998366 Age Regular Regular Medium Mature Frequent Unknown Bare soil / weeding No visible irrigation pattern Normal No 12/20/23 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsHonduras Very recently replanted plot. The shade trees are still standing, but the coffee trees have all been removed and are growing back. 35 11.62729503 107.88788 Age Regular Irregular Low Recently planted Infrequent Robusta Bare soil / weeding No visible irrigation pattern Normal No 3/6/24 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsVietnam Rows of shade trees serve as windbreak between rows of coffee trees. 47 13.856275 -89.797627 Age Regular Regular Low Replanting Frequent Unknown Bare soil / weeding No visible irrigation pattern Windbreak Yes 5/4/23 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsEl Salvador Recently replanted or stumped coffee plot. Few shade trees are still visible in the plot while the coffee plants are regrowing. 60 10.09122811 -84.16048793 Age Regular Irregular Low Stumping Frequent Arabica Bare soil / weeding No visible irrigation pattern Normal No 2/24/24 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsCosta Rica Recently planted coffee plot; the plot seems to be full-sun with no intercropping ongoing. 73 11.56012531 107.9154792 Age Regular Full sun Replanting Infrequent Robusta Bare soil / weeding No visible irrigation pattern Normal No 3/6/24 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsVietnam Recently planted coffee plot, the plot seems to be full-sun with no intercropping. 75 11.80212877 108.0328762 Age Regular Full sun Recently planted Infrequent Robusta Bare soil / weeding Visible irrigation pattern Normal No 12/9/23 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsVietnam Recently planted coffee plot. The plot seems to be full-sun with no intercropping. 98 12.0323247 108.5446739 Age Regular Full sun Recently planted Frequent Arabica Bare soil / weeding No visible irrigation pattern Normal No 1/22/22 View in Google Earth View in Google MapsVietnam",
